## EFFECTS

Ensure you follow these precise architecture and styling instructions -- do NOT replace these effects with CSS-only approximations or another library.

### [EFFECT-01] design-canvas-units
Used in: SECTION-03

Copy `lib/frame.ts` verbatim -- it is the coordinate system every absolute value in the hero depends on:

```ts
export const DESKTOP_W = 1440;
export const DESKTOP_H = 810;
export const MOBILE_W = 440;
export const MOBILE_H = 956;

export const dw = (px: number) => `${(px / DESKTOP_W) * 100}cqw`;
export const mw = (px: number) => `${(px / MOBILE_W) * 100}cqw`;

export const dpx = (percent: number, offset = 0) =>
  dw((percent / 100) * DESKTOP_W + offset);
export const dpy = (percent: number, offset = 0) =>
  dw((percent / 100) * DESKTOP_H + offset);

export const mpx = (percent: number, offset = 0) =>
  mw((percent / 100) * MOBILE_W + offset);
export const mpy = (percent: number, offset = 0) =>
  mw((percent / 100) * MOBILE_H + offset);
```

The stage element carries Tailwind's `@container` equivalent via the plain CSS class `.frame` (`container-type: size; container-name: frame`, SETUP-CSS) plus `.frame-desktop` (`width: min(100%, calc(100svh * 16 / 9)); aspect-ratio: 16 / 9`) or `.frame-mobile` (`width: min(100%, calc(100svh * 440 / 956)); aspect-ratio: 440 / 956`). Because the frame's aspect ratio is CSS-locked, `1cqw` already represents true 2-D scaling -- unlike a stage with a free-floating aspect ratio, there is no need for a second vertical-percentage helper: `dw()`/`mw()` alone size **and** position everything, `top`/`left`/`width`/`height`/`font-size`/`letter-spacing`, all through the same unit. `dpx`/`dpy`/`mpx`/`mpy` exist only to convert a Figma "percent + pixel offset" coordinate (`calc(66.67% + -5px)`) into a single `dw()`/`mw()` call.

**The three-level stage skeleton is mandatory and load-bearing.** Emit it exactly like this -- `cqw` units resolve against the nearest ancestor with `container-type`, and if the `.frame` element is missing, flattened into its parent, or replaced by a plain `<div>`, every `cqw` in the hero silently falls back to resolving against the *viewport* instead. The failure mode is not an error: the hero renders with the headline several times too large, overflowing the right edge, with the dancer and the pill "O" wildly out of register. If the hero looks "zoomed in", this skeleton is what is wrong.

```tsx
// HeroDesktop.tsx
<div ref={sectionRef} onPointerMove={…}
     className="relative isolate hidden min-h-svh w-full overflow-hidden bg-sky md:block">
  {/* backdrop lives HERE, outside the frame, so it bleeds past the letterboxed stage */}
  <motion.div aria-hidden className="absolute inset-0 -z-10" style={{ y: skyY }}>…</motion.div>

  <div className="relative flex min-h-svh items-center">
    <motion.div className="frame frame-desktop relative"
                variants={stagger(reduce, 0.075, 0.15)} initial="hidden" animate="show">
      {/* every absolutely-positioned hero layer goes here */}
    </motion.div>
  </div>
</div>
```

`HeroMobile.tsx` is the same shape with `md:hidden` on the root, `frame frame-mobile` on the stage, and `stagger(reduce, 0.07, 0.15)`. The middle `flex items-center` wrapper is what vertically centres the letterboxed frame in the viewport; the `.frame` element itself must be the direct parent of the positioned layers.

**Both hero variants are always mounted.** `components/hero/Hero.tsx` renders `<HeroDesktop />` and `<HeroMobile />` unconditionally inside one `<section id="home">`; `HeroDesktop`'s root carries `hidden ... md:block` and `HeroMobile`'s root carries `... md:hidden`. This is a pure CSS display toggle, not a `matchMedia`/`useEffect` fork -- both trees hydrate identically on server and client, so there is no flash-of-wrong-layout and no hydration-mismatch risk to guard against.

### [EFFECT-02] motion-variant-vocabulary
Used in: SECTION-01, SECTION-02, SECTION-03, SECTION-06 through SECTION-13

Copy `lib/motion.ts` verbatim -- every `variants` prop in the build (except EFFECT-04's own letter variant, which reuses `letterIn`) comes from this file:

```ts
import type { Variants } from "framer-motion";

export const EASE = [0.22, 1, 0.36, 1] as const;

export const stagger = (reduce: boolean, each = 0.08, delay = 0.1): Variants => ({
  hidden: {},
  show: {
    transition: reduce
      ? { staggerChildren: 0, delayChildren: 0 }
      : { staggerChildren: each, delayChildren: delay },
  },
});

export const riseIn = (reduce: boolean, y = 24, duration = 0.9): Variants => ({
  hidden: reduce ? { opacity: 0 } : { opacity: 0, y },
  show: {
    opacity: 1,
    y: 0,
    transition: { duration: reduce ? 0.2 : duration, ease: EASE },
  },
});

export const slideIn = (reduce: boolean, x = 32, duration = 0.9): Variants => ({
  hidden: reduce ? { opacity: 0 } : { opacity: 0, x },
  show: {
    opacity: 1,
    x: 0,
    transition: { duration: reduce ? 0.2 : duration, ease: EASE },
  },
});

export const zoomIn = (reduce: boolean, from = 1.08, duration = 1.4): Variants => ({
  hidden: reduce ? { opacity: 0 } : { opacity: 0, scale: from },
  show: {
    opacity: 1,
    scale: 1,
    transition: { duration: reduce ? 0.2 : duration, ease: EASE },
  },
});

export const curtain = (reduce: boolean, duration = 1.2): Variants => ({
  hidden: reduce ? { opacity: 0 } : { clipPath: "inset(100% 0% 0% 0%)" },
  show: {
    opacity: 1,
    clipPath: "inset(0% 0% 0% 0%)",
    transition: { duration: reduce ? 0.2 : duration, ease: EASE },
  },
});

export const curtainMedia = (reduce: boolean, duration = 1.4): Variants => ({
  hidden: reduce ? {} : { scale: 1.35 },
  show: {
    scale: 1,
    transition: { duration: reduce ? 0.2 : duration, ease: EASE },
  },
});

export const letterIn = (reduce: boolean): Variants => ({
  hidden: reduce ? { opacity: 0 } : { opacity: 0, y: "0.5em", rotate: 6 },
  show: {
    opacity: 1,
    y: 0,
    rotate: 0,
    transition: { duration: reduce ? 0.2 : 0.7, ease: EASE },
  },
});

export const overlayFade: Variants = {
  hidden: { opacity: 0 },
  show: { opacity: 1, transition: { duration: 0.35, ease: EASE } },
  exit: { opacity: 0, transition: { duration: 0.25, ease: EASE } },
};

export const passthrough: Variants = { hidden: {}, show: {} };
```

`passthrough` is not a no-op -- it is required on every wrapper `motion.div` that carries a scroll/pointer `MotionValue` in `style` (the parallax layers in EFFECT-03) while living inside a `stagger()`-driven ancestor. Without its own (empty) `hidden`/`show` keys, Framer Motion resolves the ancestor's active variant onto that node and overwrites the `y`/`x` `MotionValue` sitting in its `style` prop, breaking the parallax and producing a hydration mismatch. Every `MotionValue`-driven wrapper in the hero uses `variants={passthrough}` for exactly this reason.

### [EFFECT-03] hero-desktop-scroll-pointer-parallax
Used in: SECTION-03

Desktop hero only. Two independent, simultaneously-active mechanisms:

**Scroll depth** -- `const { scrollYProgress } = useScroll({ target: sectionRef, offset: ["start start", "end start"] })` on the hero section's own ref. Five `useTransform` reads off the same progress value, each driving a different layer's `y` (or opacity):
- sky/texture wrapper: `useTransform(scrollYProgress, [0, 1], [0, 70])`.
- halo wrapper: `[0, 1] → [0, -30]`.
- headline wrapper: `[0, 1] → [0, -150]`.
- dancer wrapper (and its frontal clip duplicate): `[0, 1] → [0, -70]`.
- stat-cards wrapper: `[0, 1] → [0, -20]`.
- headline opacity fade: `useTransform(scrollYProgress, [0, 0.8], [1, 0])`.

**Pointer drift** -- two raw `useMotionValue(0)` (`mx`, `my`), each wrapped `useSpring(value, { stiffness: 60, damping: 20, mass: 0.6 })`. `onPointerMove` on the hero root reads its own `getBoundingClientRect()` and normalises to `-0.5..0.5`: `mx.set((e.clientX - rect.left) / rect.width - 0.5)`, same formula for `my` against `rect.height`. The motion values are always created (hooks cannot be conditional), but only bound to the handler when `pointerOn = fine && !reduce` (`fine` from `usePointerFine()`, EFFECT-20's media-query hook) -- on touch the origin stays at rest. Three independent pixel ranges, each `useTransform(spring, v => v * depth)`:
- dancer: `x: v => v * -14`, `y: v => v * -8` (opposite-sign X reads as the dancer leaning away from the cursor).
- headline: `x: v => v * 9`.
- halo: `x: v => v * 22`.

Both mechanisms compose on the same layer (e.g. the dancer wrapper's `style` carries both the scroll-driven `y` and the spring-driven `x`), gated behind `variants={passthrough}` (EFFECT-02) so the stagger ancestor never clobbers them, and behind `style={reduce ? undefined : {...}}` so `prefers-reduced-motion` removes the inline transform entirely rather than animating to a zero value.

### [EFFECT-04] split-text-letter-reveal
Used in: SECTION-06 through SECTION-12 (every `SectionHead` title, plus the two Join headlines)

Copy `components/ui/SplitText.tsx` verbatim:

```tsx
"use client";

import { motion, useReducedMotion } from "framer-motion";
import { letterIn, stagger } from "@/lib/motion";

type SplitTextProps = {
  text: string;
  className?: string;
  by?: "letter" | "word";
  each?: number;
  as?: "h2" | "h3" | "p" | "span";
};

export function SplitText({
  text,
  className,
  by = "letter",
  each = 0.03,
  as = "h2",
}: SplitTextProps) {
  const reduce = useReducedMotion() ?? false;
  const Tag = motion[as] as typeof motion.h2;
  const words = text.split(" ");

  return (
    <Tag
      className={className}
      variants={stagger(reduce, each, 0)}
      initial="hidden"
      whileInView="show"
      viewport={{ once: true, margin: "-15% 0px -15% 0px" }}
    >
      <span className="sr-only">{text}</span>
      <span aria-hidden>
        {words.map((word, w) => (
          <span key={`${word}-${w}`} className="inline-block whitespace-nowrap">
            {by === "letter" ? (
              Array.from(word).map((letter, i) => (
                <span key={`${letter}-${i}`} className="inline-block overflow-hidden align-bottom">
                  <motion.span className="inline-block" variants={letterIn(reduce)}>
                    {letter}
                  </motion.span>
                </span>
              ))
            ) : (
              <span className="inline-block overflow-hidden align-bottom">
                <motion.span className="inline-block" variants={letterIn(reduce)}>
                  {word}
                </motion.span>
              </span>
            )}
            {w < words.length - 1 ? <span>&nbsp;</span> : null}
          </span>
        ))}
      </span>
    </Tag>
  );
}
```

Every visible letter/word sits inside its own `overflow-hidden` span so `letterIn`'s `y: "0.5em"` slide-up is clipped into a wipe rather than a raw translate; the full string is duplicated once as `sr-only` so screen readers and search engines read one clean sentence instead of a letter soup. `SectionHead` (used by Manifesto, Classes, Instructors, Gallery, Community, Membership) always calls it with `by="letter"` and `each={0.024}`; `Join`'s two headlines call it directly with the same default.

### [EFFECT-05] cursor-follow-preview
