Used in: SECTION-03

The "O" of "PR" is drawn as a `548.604 x 115.93`, `border-radius: 55`, `21`-unit white ring (a plain `border` box, not an SVG). Two blurred, colour-filled rectangles sit **behind** it to fake an inner shadow along the ring's own stroke, each clipped to just the ring's outline by a shared mask SVG:

```ts
// lib/assets.ts, alongside `asset()`
export const PILL_MASK =
  "data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg preserveAspectRatio='none' overflow='visible' width='548.604' height='115.93' viewBox='0 0 548.604 115.93' fill='none' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%3E%3Crect x='10.5' y='10.5' width='527.604' height='94.9297' rx='44.5' stroke='white' stroke-width='21'/%3E%3C/svg%3E";
```

`PILL_MASK` is a hand-encoded `data:` URI, **not** a bucket asset -- it must never be swapped for `asset("/svg/pill-mask.svg")`. Chrome drops `mask-image` outright when the referenced SVG is cross-origin (no console error, the layer just stops painting), so this one asset has to be embedded rather than hosted. Apply it to both shadow rectangles as:

```ts
style={{
  background: "var(--color-sky)", // second rectangle: "var(--color-slate)"
  filter: `blur(calc(${dw(24.5)} * 0.5))`,
  WebkitMaskImage: `url("${PILL_MASK}")`,
  maskImage: `url("${PILL_MASK}")`,
  maskRepeat: "no-repeat",
  WebkitMaskRepeat: "no-repeat",
  maskSize: `${dw(548.604)} ${dw(115.93)}`,
  WebkitMaskSize: `${dw(548.604)} ${dw(115.93)}`,
  maskPosition: `${dw(-182.604)} ${dw(16.125)}`, // second rectangle: -227.679 / 16.125
  WebkitMaskPosition: `${dw(-182.604)} ${dw(16.125)}`,
}}
```

The `url("${PILL_MASK}")` quotes are load-bearing, not cosmetic: `encodeURIComponent`-style data URIs can carry an unescaped `(`/`)` in their query-like segment, and an unquoted `url()` closes on the first `)` it meets, silently discarding the whole mask declaration (the symptom is the shadow rectangle rendering as a flat, unmasked color block, not as "no effect"). The two rectangles use the same mask geometry but a different `maskPosition`, sliding the visible window along the ring so each one reveals a different arc segment of the same stroke -- one reads as the shadow inside the top-left of the pill, the other as the shadow inside the bottom-right.

